Mathematical consequences asserted
----------------------------------
Translate the physical ceilings into restrictions on mathematical practice:

- only finite real precision (no infinite decimal expansions),
- finite p-adic depth,
- finite algebraic extension towers,
- finite iteration depth for algorithms,
- discretized topology (no infinite refinement of neighborhoods).

In short, every formal object is forced to be finitely describable and 
finitely approximable within the universe’s resource budget.

Whatever expansion or algorithm one writes down will be truncated by 
the global information/operations ceilings. Classical infinity has a physical 
upper bound (rather than constructing a fiat infinite mathematical surrogate).

Philosophical status
---------------------
In practice the ceilings are so enormous 10^120 – 10^12 that they have small
effect on any calculation humans or foreseeable machines will ever perform. 
They become relevant only when one asks whether an ideal infinite object can 
exist as an actual structure inside the universe.

