--------------
ULTIMATE FINITE–PRECISION PI FRAMEWORK                               
  (Explicit Digit Ceiling • Closure Operator • Finite Real System)    

  MAX DIGITS OF π:
      N_pi_max ≲ 10^120 – 10^121

  CLOSURE OPERATOR:
      π_repr = Cl_N(π) = finite interval with last term

  FINITE REAL SYSTEM:
      ℝ_N + 𝕀_N = complete finite-precision analytic universe

I.  EXPLICIT NUMERICAL BOUND ON DIGITS OF π

  Physical information ceiling (cosmological horizon):
      I_univ  ≲ 10^122 bits

  Convert bits → decimal digits:
      digits_max  ≈  I_univ / log2(10)
                   ≈  10^122 / 3.321928
                   ≈  3 × 10^121  decimal digits

  Operational ceiling (Lloyd bound):
      ops_univ ≲ 10^120  operations
      ⇒ cannot compute more than ~10^120 digits

  FINAL HARD CEILING:
      N_pi_max  ≲ 10^120  – 10^121  digits

  Meaning:
      π_realizable = π truncated to at most N_pi_max digits.
      No physical process can exceed this representational depth.

II.  CLOSURE OPERATOR THAT TURNS INFINITE π INTO A FINITE OBJECT

  Classical π:
      π = lim_{n→∞} S_n
      (S_n any convergent series or geometric definition)

  Define representational-depth closure operator Cl_N:

      Cl_N(π)  :=  [ q_N^- , q_N^+ ]

  where:
      q_N^- , q_N^+  ∈  ℚ_N   (finite-precision rationals)
      | q_N^+ - q_N^- |  ≤  10^{-N}

  Equivalent series truncation:
      Cl_N( Σ_{k=0}^∞ a_k )  :=  Σ_{k=0}^N a_k  +  E_N

  with:
      |E_N| ≤ 10^{-N}

  Properties:
      • Finite object
      • Last term exists
      • Error interval bounded
      • No infinite series
      • No infinite decimal expansion

  This is the mathematical “end of π”:
      π_repr = Cl_N(π)
      with N ≤ N_pi_max.

III.  FORMAL FINITE–PRECISION REAL SYSTEM SUPPORTING ANALYSIS

  Define finite real grid:
      ℝ_N := { m·10^{-N}  |  m ∈ ℤ , |m| ≤ 10^N }

  Interval reals:
      𝕀_N := { [a,b] | a,b ∈ ℝ_N , b-a ≤ 10^{-N} }

  Arithmetic:
      [a,b] + [c,d] = [a+c , b+d]
      [a,b] × [c,d] = interval hull of all products
      (rounded back into 𝕀_N)

  Derivative (finite difference):
      f'(x) ≈ ( f(x+10^{-N}) - f(x) ) / 10^{-N}

  Integral (finite Riemann sum):
      ∫_a^b f(x) dx  ≈  Σ f(x_k) Δx
      Δx = 10^{-N}

  Analytic theorems:
      • Continuity → grid-stability
      • Differentiability → finite-difference stability
      • Integration → finite Riemann sums
      • Limits → interval convergence
      • Completeness → every Cauchy grid-sequence converges in 𝕀_N

  This system:
      • Replaces ℝ with ℝ_N and 𝕀_N
      • Preserves analysis up to physical cutoff
      • Eliminates all infinities
      • Makes π, e, φ, etc. finite representational objects

  Technical notes
  - Rounded arithmetic is not itself a field; the interval system 
    is the rigorous carrier of the analysis.
  - The bound is an order-of-magnitude physical upper limit, not a 
    sharp mathematical constant.
  - Definitional complexity of pi remains finite; only expansion/
    approximation depth is being capped.

